Change-ID: CL-20260803-133
Commit: pending
Date: 2026-08-03
Type: Feature -- refinement edits by patch, and stops echoing files into prompts
Request-or-TODO: Direct instruction, 2026-08-03: diff-based edits, structured
  and targeted, and stop echoing whole files
Outcome: Two changes, and the second was the more expensive defect.
  UserReadableToolSummary rendered every argument in full, and that summary
  travels back into the next prompt as the record of what the last round did.
  So a written file's entire contents were echoed into every subsequent request
  for the rest of the run — two or three thousand bytes per edit, repeated,
  paid for by the token and crowding out the instruction. Large values are now
  a bounded excerpt with their length and digest. What the next round needs is
  that the write happened, to what, and how big it was; if it needs the
  contents it can read them, which costs the same tokens once instead of every
  round.
  apply-patch is a real tool now, taking the format the models already reach
  for: a "*** Update File" header, "@@" before each hunk, lines to remove
  prefixed "-", lines to add prefixed "+", and unprefixed context around them.
  A hunk is located by its whole before-text rather than by line numbers,
  because a line number goes stale the moment anything above it moves and a
  mis-numbered hunk either fails obscurely or applies in the wrong place.
  Context does not go stale: it either still reads that way or it does not.
  Every hunk must match exactly once. Matching twice is not a near miss to
  resolve by guessing — it is the patch failing to say which place is meant,
  and guessing is how an edit lands in the wrong function. Every hunk applies
  or none does, and the result must still parse as Go before anything is
  written, because a half-patched file is a state neither the model nor the
  coordinator asked for.
  The whole-file tool now routes patch syntax rather than refusing it: the
  content was right and the tool was wrong, and saying which tool takes it is
  worth more than explaining what a Go file looks like. Its summary says it is
  for creating a file or replacing one wholesale, and the house style says to
  patch an existing file rather than rewrite it.
Verification: Build, vet, gofmt, seven new patch tests covering ambiguity,
  staleness, all-or-nothing application, missing context, the frame, and
  git-style headers
Dev-Log: DL-20260803-143

Change-ID: CL-20260803-132
Commit: pending
Date: 2026-08-03
Type: Fix -- the write tool described itself as a patch API, and got patches
Request-or-TODO: Observed live in the rung 6 trace, 2026-08-03
Outcome: The trace paid for itself on its first run. Rung 6 spent four turns
  sending "*** Begin Patch" three times and "@@" once, each refused, and the
  reason was not the model. The tool's own summary read "Apply an expected-hash
  edit batch", which is a patch API described in words. A model reads the
  summary before deciding what to send, and it sent what the summary described.
  The summary now says what the tool takes: one file's complete new contents,
  not a patch or diff. A test asserts it keeps saying so, because this is
  exactly the kind of wording that drifts back.
  The refusal names the mistake too. "What arrived is not source at all" does
  not tell a model which of its habits to drop; "this tool has no patch mode,
  send the whole file from its package clause to its last line" does. Detected
  from a fixed list of openings — the unified-diff hunk and file headers, the
  apply-patch envelope markers, a merge conflict marker — rather than guessed
  from content, so a Go file that starts oddly is not misread.
Verification: Build, vet, gofmt, the full executor suite including two new
  tests
Dev-Log: DL-20260803-142

Change-ID: CL-20260803-131
Commit: pending
Date: 2026-08-03
Type: Fix -- the phase trace said nothing, and the ladder's second question was
  never asked
Request-or-TODO: Observed in the rung 6 trace, 2026-08-03
Outcome: The phase boundaries added an hour earlier were nonsense on their
  first real run. Stages are decided in dependency order rather than in numeric
  order, so the label changed constantly and the run reported twenty-three
  phases in four minutes, bouncing between specification and delivery. A
  boundary every time a label changes is not a phase; it is a label changing.
  A phase is now announced once, the first time one of its stages is decided,
  and the totals are reported at the end with each phase's share. The most
  useful line is the remainder: rung 6 measured nine seconds of stages across
  four minutes, and the answer to "why is this slow" was never going to be in
  any stage — it is the model calls and tool runs between them, which the trace
  now says outright rather than leaving a reader to subtract.
  The ladder's second question was also missing: an edit adding it had silently
  failed to apply, so the suite still reported only whether the program worked.
  It now logs the task state and fails when the program is correct and the
  pipeline did not converge.
Verification: Build, vet, gofmt, the coordinator suites
Dev-Log: DL-20260803-141

Change-ID: CL-20260803-130
Commit: pending
Date: 2026-08-03
Type: Feature -- three ways a run can go missing, closed
Request-or-TODO: Provider-failure analysis, items 7, 9 and 10
Outcome: A run may not return with its task active, and that is now enforced by
  a defer rather than intended by every path. "Every path arranges it" is the
  kind of claim that stays true until somebody adds a path, and a task left
  running is invisible: nothing retries it, nothing reports it, and whatever is
  waiting on it waits forever. The forced ending is recovery-required rather
  than failed, because a run that reached the end without recording an outcome
  produced work nobody has judged.
  The case a defer cannot catch — the process died, so there was no return to
  defer from — is covered by a reconciler that finds tasks sitting active with
  nothing happening to them and marks them for recovery. It never resumes
  anything: restarting work that may have been half done, on a provider that
  may have been why it died, is a decision with a bill attached and belongs to
  a person. Its staleness window is deliberately generous, because reconciling
  a live run out from under itself is a far worse defect than leaving a dead
  one for another cycle.
  Provider health is now shared across runs. The per-run circuit is right and
  insufficient: twenty tasks discovering the same dead provider one full
  timeout at a time is twenty times the wait and twenty times the spend for one
  fact. A run that exhausts a provider records it for thirty seconds; a run
  starting inside that window is told before it waits rather than after. It is
  advisory — the run still tries, because a task that never starts because an
  unrelated one failed is the worse mistake — and a success clears it at once.
Verification: Build, vet, gofmt, six new tests covering the shared health
  window and the reconciler's refusal to touch a live run
Dev-Log: DL-20260803-140

Change-ID: CL-20260803-129
Commit: pending
Date: 2026-08-03
Type: Fix -- provider failure becomes a bounded state machine, and the trace
  shows phases, timings, prompts and replies
Request-or-TODO: Provider-failure analysis and the tracing request, 2026-08-03
Outcome: Opening the circuit was tied to holding a checkpoint, so the case with
  no verified work -- the one where stopping matters most, because there is
  nothing to fall back on -- was the case that kept asking an unavailable
  provider. The checkpoint now decides what recovery means, not whether the
  breaker opens.
  Four dispositions replace one boolean, because the three ways of stopping are
  genuinely different: a run holding verified work finishes on it, a run
  holding an unverified draft is left recovery-required for somebody to resume,
  and a run whose credentials are wrong is not waiting for anything.
  recovery-required is not "failed": a draft nobody could check because the
  machinery went away is unfinished work, not bad work, and it is the only
  ending that says the worktree is worth keeping.
  The machinery got its own allowance, separate from the work's and never
  refunded. Refunding the work's budget is right -- a run learned nothing from
  an outage -- but a refund with no separate ceiling is an unbounded licence to
  keep asking. One outer retry and twenty-five seconds; the provider adapter
  already owns network retries with its own backoff, and this layer's only job
  is to notice that the layer below has given up.
  Cancellation outranks everything, and an authentication or safety refusal is
  not retried at all. The message is composed from the ruling, so a run can no
  longer say "Trying again" and finalise on the next line.
  Finalisation runs on its own context, derived with WithoutCancel: the context
  that timed out waiting for a model was the same one used to write the ending,
  so a run that died of a provider timeout could not record that it had.
  The trace now shows what a watcher asked for: phase boundaries with elapsed
  time, per-stage duration, and the prompts and replies themselves. The trace
  primitives moved to internal/tracing so the model client can write to the
  same stream -- a trace showing which stage is running and not what was sent
  answers "where is it" and not "why".
Verification: Build, vet, gofmt, seven circuit tests covering exhaustion with
  and without a checkpoint, the bounded transport retry, the unrefundable
  allowance, unretryable failures, cancellation precedence and the narration
Dev-Log: DL-20260803-139

Change-ID: CL-20260803-128
Commit: pending
Date: 2026-08-03
Type: Fix -- the refinement policy stops spending runs on invented wording
Request-or-TODO: Rung 6 trace analysis, 2026-08-03, items 1 to 8, 10 and 11
Outcome: Most of rung 6's churn went on error prose the requirement never
  specified -- "expression leaves 0 values", then "... on stack", then "... on
  the stack" -- and on a test demanding `operator "a" requires two operands`
  where the implementation's `invalid token "a"` was the better answer. The
  style directive now says it plainly: assert that a failure happened and that
  it is the sentinel or type the code declares, never the exact text of a
  message the request did not specify. The wording is the implementation's to
  choose, and a test that pins it turns a correct program into a failing one
  for a reason nobody asked about.
  A parameter a function parses now gets a parser's questions -- input that
  runs out part way, input that is well formed and then continues, a token the
  grammar has no meaning for -- instead of the collection heuristics. An RPN
  token stream is ordered syntax; "the same element repeated" is not a tie to
  break and "already in the wrong order" is not a property it has.
  Property tests joined the attempt loop. Rung 6 ended on "atom-property-tests
  failed" having never been asked for one: the gate was hard, the run had
  attempts left, and nothing told it. Path coverage was decoupled from the
  six-link chain that had it waiting on property tests, atoms, verification,
  composition and control flow -- it runs the suite itself, so what it needs is
  something to run, not somebody else's verdict on having run it.
  A blind-spot round may now write only test files, and a documentation round
  may change only comments, enforced at the write with the executable syntax
  tree compared before and after. Both were already asked for in prose, and
  rung 6 was asked for a comment on main and rewrote the evaluator's error
  semantics.
  Content that is not source is refused by its shape rather than by a column
  number. "<<", "*", "noop" and "#" arrived as Go source across two attempts
  and each got "expected 'package', found ..." -- the symptom, where the run
  needed the category.
  "Trying again" is no longer said by a run whose circuit has just opened, and
  "ready to be looked at with advisories" is reserved for a run with no hard
  failure. The ladder now reports the task state beside the program's
  behaviour and fails when they disagree: rung 6 reported PASS while the task
  ended failed, which reads as "the platform worked" when what was established
  was "the program was correct despite the platform not finishing".
Verification: Build, vet, gofmt, thirteen new tests across edit scope, the
  write shape check, case duplication and reader matching
Dev-Log: DL-20260803-138

Change-ID: CL-20260803-127
Commit: pending
Date: 2026-08-03
Type: Fix -- two cases stop being one, and the schema stops claiming what it
  cannot know
Request-or-TODO: Rung 5 enrichment analysis, items 5 and 7
Outcome: sampleOf falls back to the zero value for any type it does not know,
  so a slice of a produced struct rendered its "three distinct items" case and
  its "the same item repeated" case as the identical text, and its
  one-element case and its zero-valued-element case likewise. One label of each
  pair is not what it says, no suite can answer both from one literal, and
  counting them separately asked for a case that does not exist. Where no two
  distinct values can be written the cases are now described rather than
  quoted, and every type's ladder is deduplicated on the way out, keeping the
  case whose class matters most -- if a run only gets to try one input, that is
  the one to try.
  The derived documentation stopped making claims the analysis cannot support.
  A function handed an io.Reader is pure by the effect analysis, because it
  reaches nothing outside its arguments, and calling it twice does not give the
  same answer: the reader is consumed. Writing "the same arguments give the
  same results" of it is confidently false, and a later run reusing it on that
  basis is wrong in a way the documentation caused. Determinism and idempotency
  now read unknown for a function taking a stream, complexity reads unknown
  where the cost is dominated by something this cannot measure -- a JSON decode
  is not constant time because the body has no loop -- and verification says
  the acceptance examples are properties of the whole command rather than of
  this function alone. A genuinely pure function keeps its confident answers:
  the point is accuracy, not timidity.
Verification: Build, vet, gofmt, four new tests
Dev-Log: DL-20260803-137

Change-ID: CL-20260803-126
Commit: pending
Date: 2026-08-03
Type: Fix -- enrichment commits or does not happen, and unverified atoms are
  not admitted
Request-or-TODO: Rung 5 enrichment analysis, 2026-08-03, items 1 to 4 and 10
Outcome: Deriving the registry documentation was right; doing it in place was
  not. Enrichment edited the verified worktree, revalidated, and stopped --
  leaving the tree different from every recorded artifact and from the
  checkpoint digest the run then reported as its verified revision. Rung 5
  ended with a correct 7,593-byte program on disk, an artifact record holding
  the version before enrichment, and a terminal record saying "the worktree is
  NOT verified revision 8bef148ac7cd". All three were accurate, and the run
  failed anyway, because the only thing anyone could read back was the stale
  copy.
  The sequence now commits or does not happen: enrich, build, test, check the
  acceptance examples, re-record every produced file, capture a new verified
  digest. Any step failing puts the previous revision back and records nothing.
  Registration is withheld when this run's atom verification was performed and
  did not hold. Rung 5 registered three atoms while case synthesis had failed,
  example tests and verification were blocked behind it, and the task ended
  failed. A registry row asserts that a later task may reuse this instead of
  building it, and saying that about code whose own verification never ran
  poisons every project that reads it.
  Withheld on a checked failure rather than on the absence of a check. A flow
  that never reached the stage has established nothing either way, and refusing
  on that would stop registration everywhere except a full run.
Verification: Build, vet, gofmt, the recall, registration and derived-schema
  suites
Dev-Log: DL-20260803-136

Change-ID: CL-20260803-125
Commit: pending
Date: 2026-08-03
Type: Fix -- a blind spot asks for a test, and a run keeps time to finish
Request-or-TODO: Rung 5 regression-stall analysis, items 3 and 5
Outcome: A review that found only blind spots is asking for tests, not for
  edits. A blind spot is behaviour nothing checks, which is not the same as
  behaviour that is wrong, and the program is usually already correct about it:
  a parser asked for an empty-input test very often rejects empty input
  perfectly well and has simply never been asked. Ladder rung 5 was given two
  blind spots, rewrote its production code to address them, changed its output
  in the process, and spent four attempts getting back to a program it already
  had. The order is now stated: write the test, run it, change the program only
  for a test that actually fails, and do not rewrite working code to make room
  for a test. A review holding a real defect is told none of this, because a
  demonstrated defect is not closed by a test that passes.
  A run also now keeps a minute of wall clock back to finish with. What
  finishing costs is knowable -- restore the checkpoint, rebuild, re-run the
  suite, check acceptance, write the terminal state -- and a run that spends
  its last minute on a model call it cannot complete has no result, no terminal
  state, and a record that says nothing, while a verified revision sits on
  disk. Only when the caller set a deadline: a run without one has nothing to
  reserve against, and inventing a limit would end runs entitled to continue.
Verification: Build, vet, gofmt, one new test that a blind-spot-only review
  asks for tests first and a defect-bearing one does not
Dev-Log: DL-20260803-135

Change-ID: CL-20260803-124
Commit: pending
Date: 2026-08-03
Type: Fix -- a failed refinement stops becoming the next refinement's baseline
Request-or-TODO: Rung 5 regression-stall analysis, 2026-08-03
Outcome: A refinement is a candidate, not a commit, and every attempt was
  editing the worktree in place -- so a failed candidate became the next
  candidate's baseline. Ladder rung 5 had a correct, tested, accepted program
  at 33.8 seconds, changed its output while adding two blind-spot tests, and
  then spent attempts three, four and five repairing a descendant of the broken
  version: losing a function its own tests named, drifting a signature, and
  referencing an undefined identifier, none of which existed in the program it
  started from.
  A refinement that loses the build, the tests or the acceptance examples is
  now discarded and the verified revision put back before the next attempt. The
  failed candidate is preserved beside the worktree rather than deleted -- what
  it tried is a hypothesis that has been run, and it is the most useful thing
  the next attempt can be told -- and the instruction says plainly that the
  edits are gone and why, because a run that reads its own files and finds work
  it does not remember doing will assume the tooling is broken.
  Nothing is discarded when there is no verified revision to go back to. A run
  that has never had one has nothing better than what it is holding.
  Separately, an exhausted retry budget now opens the circuit on its first
  occurrence. The two-failure rule is right for a raw transport error, which
  may be one closed socket, and wrong for "retry budget exhausted" -- that is
  the provider's own retry policy reporting it has already tried and given up.
  Rung 5 waited 90.8 seconds for the first and began a second attempt that
  would have cost another ninety, with a verified checkpoint on disk the whole
  time.
  A gate that a run had satisfied and then lost now escalates on its second
  failure rather than its third. A cycle is by definition not consecutive: the
  failures alternate with the fixes, which is what makes it a cycle and what
  made it invisible to a consecutive-repeat counter.
Verification: Build, vet, gofmt, two new discard tests
Dev-Log: DL-20260803-134

Change-ID: CL-20260803-123
Commit: pending
Date: 2026-08-03
Type: Fix -- a restore is honest, and an advisory stage stops gating hard ones
Request-or-TODO: Rung 5 trace analysis, 2026-08-03, items 1 to 3
Outcome: A restore now clears everything the run believed about the tree it
  replaced -- whether validation ran, whether it failed, whether files moved
  since, the last suite fingerprint, the last failure -- rather than reasoning
  around stale flags. A stale fact that survives a restore is
  indistinguishable from a current one, and the whole point of the restore is
  that the worktree changed.
  The terminal record states three facts separately where one boolean used to
  answer all three badly: whether the tree that exists now passes the
  completion floor, whether every required gate held, and whether this tree is
  the one the checkpoint verified. Rung 5 restored a verified revision and
  reported "the work was never verified" -- true of the gates, false of the
  tree, and said as though it were both.
  A hard stage may no longer depend on an advisory one. Composition
  obligations required atom documentation, which was reasonable while
  documentation was asked of the model during the run and is not now that it
  is derived after verification and classified advisory. The edge blocked four
  hard stages -- composition obligations, molecule tests, molecules, molecule
  verification -- so a run with a perfectly good composition was told nothing
  about it because a comment was missing. The dependency that survives is the
  real one: obligations are stated over atoms shown to work.
Verification: Build, vet, gofmt, the finalization suite and the pipeline suite
Dev-Log: DL-20260803-133

Change-ID: CL-20260803-122
Commit: pending
Date: 2026-08-03
Type: Fix -- a restored run stops calling its own verified work unverified
Request-or-TODO: Rung 5, run alone, 2026-08-03; analysis item 12
Outcome: Rung 5 passed, and the checkpoint did exactly what it is for: attempt
  6 broke the suite, the run put back revision 28c3375abe72, and finished on
  work that had compiled, passed its tests and matched its acceptance examples.
  Its record then said "Final status: failed — the work was never verified",
  in the same paragraph as "restored=true verified_revision=28c3375abe72",
  about the same worktree.
  filesChangedSinceValidation tracks what the agent wrote, so a restore was
  invisible to it: the run replaced the tree and then read the failed verdict
  of the attempt that had broken it. A restore is a write, and the verdict has
  to describe the tree that exists, so it now forces a revalidation like any
  other write.
