Dev-Log: DL-20260803-131

Change-ID: CL-20260803-120
Commit: pending
Date: 2026-08-03
Type: Fix -- the three blockers: fatal loop errors, late gates, and a noise gate
Request-or-TODO: Process-refinement analysis, 2026-08-03, items 1 to 4
Outcome: A loop error this code had no name for ended the run outright. Two
  named errors were absorbed and everything else was fatal, which put "the loop
  refused a tool result whose identity it could not match" in the same category
  as "the database will not open". Rung 3 died on exactly that, having already
  written a working program and needing one more attempt to add a missing
  import; thirty-seven stages were recorded as never performed because of one
  refused turn. Unrecognised loop errors now cost an attempt, bounded at two,
  after which the run ends with the error -- which is correct for a machine
  that is actually broken.
  Path coverage was measured only after the attempt loop, so a run converged,
  finished, and was then told that five of its thirty-nine changed lines are
  executed by nothing. Rung 2 did exactly that. Uncovered lines are now named
  inside the loop, while attempts remain, with the lines listed rather than a
  percentage: a run told "coverage is 87%" has to work out which lines the
  number is about before it can act. Bounded at three rounds, because coverage
  of generated code has a floor below which the remaining lines are error
  branches nothing reaches without contorting the program.
  The non-functional gate stopped reporting weather as a defect. A ratio alone
  measures noise when the baseline is small: rung 2 failed at 591ms against a
  372ms baseline. The limit is now the larger of the 1.5x ratio and the
  baseline plus half a second, so a minute-long suite is unaffected and a
  third-of-a-second suite stops crying wolf. It also joins the advisory set,
  where a wall-clock measurement on a developer machine belongs.
  Severity is printed beside every stage decision. A reader watching a run
  could not otherwise tell a failure that stops the work from one carried as a
  note, and the two look identical in a log.
Verification: Build, vet, gofmt, the finalization and non-functional tests
Dev-Log: DL-20260803-130

Change-ID: CL-20260803-119
Commit: pending
Date: 2026-08-03
Type: Fix -- a saving of one test run cost a whole rung
Request-or-TODO: Observed in the shared rungs 1-3 run, 2026-08-03
Outcome: CL-20260803-114 refused a test run against files that had not moved
  since the last one, by returning a result without calling the executor. The
  executor is what records a tool in the run's journal, so the loop saw a turn
  whose tool never happened, refused it, and ended the run. Ladder rung 3 lost
  all thirty-seven of its remaining stages -- every one recorded
  "not-implemented" -- to a saving of one test run.
  The observation was right and the mechanism was wrong. The tool runs, and the
  output carries the note: not one byte of the produced files changed since the
  last time these tests ran, so this result is that result, and if you meant to
  change something the write did not land. That is a fact the model cannot see
  for itself, and it is the one that makes the next step obvious.
  Also recorded for the ladder: rungs 1 and 2 passed in the same shared root,
  and the memory preflight found 0, then 1, then 2 candidates across the three
  rungs -- the first time work has accumulated across tasks in this project.
