Change-ID: CL-20260803-115
Commit: pending
Date: 2026-08-03
Type: Fix -- two gates that could not be satisfied by any correct program
Request-or-TODO: Refinement analysis items 4 and 7, both observed live on rung 3
Outcome: The adversarial critic told main it had swallowed an error by
  returning none of its own. Go defines main as func main(), with no results; a
  main that returned an error would not compile. Rung 3 was told this twice per
  attempt, could not comply, was sent back three times for the same finding,
  escalated to the most expensive rung on the resulting stall, and spent the
  rest of its budget there before the provider gave out. A gate whose
  instruction cannot be followed does not merely fail to improve the work -- it
  consumes the run.
  What main genuinely owes is now checked instead, and it is satisfiable: a
  main that calls something fallible and then exits zero whatever happens has
  produced a program that fails silently and reports success to whatever ran
  it. Write the message to os.Stderr and call os.Exit(1). A main that already
  exits non-zero, and a main that calls nothing fallible, owe nothing.
  Separately, math.MaxInt is no longer proposed for a function whose work is
  proportional to its argument. Rung 3 is FizzBuzz -- its whole body is a loop
  bounded by that argument -- and the case cannot finish. The boundary still
  gets a case, bounded, because "well past what the author pictured" is worth
  asking and "a run that cannot finish" is not. A function with no loop keeps
  its overflow case, which is a fair question there.
  The two invented rationales are gone: "ties must be broken somehow" now reads
  as duplicate handling being decided rather than assumed, which is what the
  case was ever actually about.
