Change-ID: CL-20260803-097
Commit: pending
Date: 2026-08-03
Type: Fix -- refuse non-Go in a .go file, and stop demanding a test twice
Request-or-TODO: The ladder-ascent instruction, 2026-08-03
Outcome: Two more defects found by climbing rung two, both costing whole
  attempts of a run that was otherwise converging.
  The write tool wrote whatever it was handed and reported the byte count. On
  one run the model twice wrote a file whose first character was an asterisk,
  the build failed with "expected 'package', found '*'", and each occurrence
  cost a write, a build, a send-back and a full rewrite. One landed on the
  run's last attempt, so every structural stage recorded blocked because the
  module did not build. A .go write is now parsed before it lands and refused
  with a message the model can act on in the same round.
  Only the parse is checked. Code that will not compile, names something
  undefined, or carries an unused import is still written, because that is the
  compiler's business and the flow's; refusing it here would turn a guard on
  the file into an opinion about the code. The content is refused rather than
  repaired: stripping a fence would hide the defect from the run that caused it
  and leave the model no reason to stop producing it. Files under testdata are
  exempt by the same convention the Go toolchain uses.
  Separately, the case gate counted an empty slice and a nil slice as two
  demands. A function that ranges over a slice cannot tell []string{} from a
  nil []string -- both have length zero and both yield nothing -- so a run that
  wrote {name: "empty", args: nil} was told the empty input was never tried and
  sent back to write the test it had already written
Affected-behavior: A malformed .go write fails at the tool with a specific
  reason instead of at the build with a parse error attributed to the run. The
  case gate stops asking for a duplicate test
Compatibility-or-migration: None
Verification: The write guard is proven against four real shapes of malformed
  output -- a markdown heading, a fenced block, a diff marker and prose before
  the source -- with a control admitting code that parses but will not compile,
  and a control for the testdata and non-Go exemptions. The nil equivalence is
  proven both ways: nil covers the empty case, and says nothing about the
  single-element or duplicate cases
Known-limitations: The nil scan is textual and asks whether the suite ever
  supplies nil, not whether it supplies it to this parameter. Resolving that
  properly means the call graph, which is the mutation stage's job, and the
  failure mode of being shallow here is far cheaper than telling a run to
  rewrite a test it already has
