Date: 2026-08-03
Type: Fix -- a run can no longer be stranded, and the ledger describes the run
Request-or-TODO: The ladder-ascent instruction, 2026-08-03
Outcome: Climbing from rung one to rung two exposed four defects, three of them
  in the platform rather than in anything a model wrote.
  A run whose agent returned an error was stranded. The error was discarded at
  the call site, so the task sat in running with an idle worker on the other end
  of a loopback connection until something timed out half an hour later, and
  nothing anywhere recorded why. Establishing that took a goroutine dump and a
  process listing: the worker had burned zero seconds of CPU and had no
  connection to any provider. The error is now acted on and the task reaches a
  terminal state, which turned a silent thirty-minute hang into a forty-second
  failure that named its own cause.
  What it named was the second defect. PersistPlanStepTransition is the port the
  loop reports through; storage owns plan-step state and promotes steps itself
  when it records validation attributions, so the loop's account routinely
  arrives after the fact. A stale-revision refusal aborted the whole run. Two
  narrower rules were tried first -- already at the target state, then already
  past it along the forward chain -- and both still failed on real runs, so the
  rule is now the general one: a reporting port may not end a run by disagreeing
  with the record it reports into.
  Third, atom-case-synthesis demanded that a test contain the synthesiser's own
  invented literals. []string{"a", "b", "c"} does not mean the letters a, b and
  c, it means three distinct strings, and searching the test source for that
  exact text asked for a coincidence. Rung two recorded ten of eighteen cases
  never tried for a program that was correct, built, ran, and survived the
  adversarial probe. Matching is now structural -- same type, same cardinality
  class, same repeats-or-not -- and scalars are still matched literally because
  nil, "" and 0 mean exactly themselves. That took the same run to four of nine,
  and those four are real gaps the tests genuinely do not cover.
  Fourth, the flow ledger described the first draft rather than the run. It was
  built once per Run while Run loops over attempts, and storage keeps the first
  row for a task, attempt and stage, so every attempt after the first was
  discarded. A run that converged after six attempts still read as whatever
  failed on attempt one -- not merely incomplete, but disagreeing with the
  outcome the same run reported
Affected-behavior: A stranded run becomes a failed one. A converged run's ledger
  now shows the attempt that converged. The case gate is satisfiable
Compatibility-or-migration: Stages decided before the attempt loop are sealed
  and re-recorded under each later attempt rather than left out. Recording them
  as never performed was the first thing the ledger change got wrong, and it is
  written down here rather than quietly corrected
Verification: Each gate fix has a discrimination test verified by reverting and
  reproducing the exact recorded failure, and a control keeping the gate firing
  for the case it exists to catch. The strand and the race were verified against
  real ladder runs rather than fixtures: same binary, same rung, hang before and
  a named failure after
Known-limitations: The transition row is not written when the report is late, so
  the ledger holds storage's promotion without the loop's account of it. A gap
  in the narrative, not in the state.
  A separate code-generation defect is observed and unfixed: the model twice
  wrote a file beginning with an asterisk, giving "expected 'package', found
  '*'". The run recovered both times, so the write path is accepting output that
  is not Go source
