Change-ID: CL-20260802-092
Commit: pending
Date: 2026-08-02
Type: Security -- a mediated command's descendants no longer outlive it
Request-or-TODO: PIPE-132a, PIPE-133a
Outcome: Every mediated subprocess is now assigned to a Windows job object that
  kills its members when the handle closes, released on every exit path.
  The gap this actually closes had not been named anywhere: the pre-existing
  process-tree kill ran only from the timeout and cancellation branches, so a
  mediated command that exited successfully -- the ordinary case -- never had
  its descendants reaped at all. A helper started rather than waited for kept
  running, unconfined, indefinitely. Closing the job now terminates every
  process ever assigned to it at any depth, including one that asks to break
  away, because neither breakaway flag is set.
  PIPE-133a's two named observations stay open, and forcing them to pass was
  refused. A job object bounds what outlives a command; it has no bearing on
  what a still-running process does, and both actions in the original abuse case
  complete synchronously before mediation is finished with the command. Those
  two recorded lines are left exactly as they were
Affected-behavior: A background process a verification command leaves behind is
  now killed when that command completes. Anything relying on such a process
  surviving would break, and nothing does
Compatibility-or-migration: golang.org/x/sys/windows was already a dependency at
  v0.46.0; no new dependency. A no-op sibling keeps the Unix call sites
  compiling with behaviour unchanged
Verification: The discriminating test was verified by disabling confinement and
  reproducing the failure, then restoring it -- not by assertion. A mediated
  command spawns a helper that sleeps and then writes a marker and returns
  immediately; the marker never appears, and an unmediated control proves the
  same helper does survive without confinement. A third abuse vector was added
  to PIPE-133's own family rather than rewriting it: a detached helper betting
  on outliving mediation, which this mechanism does refuse
Known-limitations: Assignment happens just after the process starts rather than
  through suspended creation, so there is a small window during which the child
  runs outside the job. A running mediated process can still open a socket to
  anywhere and write to any absolute path; closing that needs network filtering
  or a filesystem jail, which this codebase does not have on Windows.
  Measured cost by the same disable-and-restore method: the engine canary moves
  from 3.91-4.13s to 4.13-4.74s, about ten per cent, on a machine six lanes were
  sharing -- real but not lab-clean
