  where declaration and body agree holds. A vacuity guard confirms the
  no-declaration case is skipped, not held
Known-limitations: A call through a value that cannot be resolved to an import
  -- a method on a local variable, a struct field, an injected interface -- is
  invisible to the effect resolver. The wider rule was tried and rejected: it
  flags var b strings.Builder; b.WriteString(x) as impure, trading a stated
  false negative for a much larger false positive across ordinary code. That is
  a judgment call rather than a proof. Preconditions and postconditions actually
  holding are still unchecked
