Change-ID: CL-20260802-077
Commit: pending
Date: 2026-08-02
Type: Fix -- the mutation score now measures what it claims
Request-or-TODO: PIPE-127, PIPE-128, PIPE-129, PIPE-130
Outcome: The score was inflatable three ways and unverified in a fourth.
  An uncompilable mutant counted as caught, so the cheapest way to raise the
  score was to generate mutants that could not build. Each candidate is now
  built before the suite ever runs and discarded if it fails, with its own
  counter. On a real fixture, one of the old implementation's five "caught"
  mutations was a build failure it credited as caught -- the free score
  observed rather than theorised. Discarding is also about six times cheaper
  than scoring: 0.11s against 0.73s, and the ratio widens on a slower suite.
  Mutation now walks the syntax tree. Only *ast.BinaryExpr nodes with a mutable
  operator are candidates, spliced at the operator's own position, so landing
  inside a string literal or a comment is structurally impossible rather than
  avoided by a rule -- a literal is one opaque node and a comment is wired into
  no expression.
  Sampling takes an evenly spaced stride across the attributed candidates rather
  than the first twelve, so the score describes the change instead of whichever
  lines come first
Affected-behavior: The reported score changes, and should be read as more
  honest rather than as a regression where it drops
Compatibility-or-migration: StageAtomMutation's PIPE-127 unestablished marker is
  cleared, on the basis that the gate claims the tests are known to detect
  defects and the score is now shown to discriminate
Verification: PIPE-130 is the proof and is the reason the rest is believable. A
  fixture whose only test discards its result -- genuinely blind to the
  boundary -- scores 0% and records broke, so a high score is unachievable there
  by construction. The contrast is identical source with a test that checks both
  edges, scoring 100%. Without that pairing a low score would be
  indistinguishable from a broken check.
  PIPE-128's proof is a contrast in one test: the same " == " byte pattern is
  placed in a comment, a string literal and real code, and the retained byte
  scanner is run beside the AST discovery. The AST finds only the real code; the
  scanner lands on the comment
Known-limitations: PIPE-130's full text also asks for false-positive and
  false-negative rates, which need repeated trials across a fixture population.
  Only the discrimination proof was delivered; the rates are filed as PIPE-130a,
  and until they exist the threshold is a number nobody has calibrated
